// ellipgears — a pair of elliptical gears: the classic non-circular gear pair,
// which turns steady input into a surging output. Two identical ellipses
// (semi-axes 50 mm and 43.3 mm, eccentricity 0.5), each pivoted at one focus,
// with the shafts 2a = 100 mm apart. That is the one arrangement in which an
// ellipse pair rolls on itself: the contact point always lies on the line of
// centers at distance r_A from one shaft and r_B = 2a - r_A from the other.
//
// The output is not a steady fraction of the input. Where gear A's radius is
// largest and B's smallest, B turns (1+e)/(1-e) = 3 times faster than A; half a
// turn later the ratio is 1/3. Over a full turn the average is exactly 1:1.
//
//   mech check examples/ellipgears.mech
//   mech run   examples/ellipgears.mech --headless 4
//
// `mesh` takes two profiles in place of two gears. The compiler checks that the
// pair is *conjugate* — that at every angle the two curves meet tangentially —
// and refuses one that is not, since such a pair would cross rather than roll.
mechanism ellipgears {
  ground frame {
    anchor pa at (0, 0)
    anchor pb at (0.1, 0)
  }

  shaft a at frame.pa
  shaft b at frame.pb

  profile ga on a.A { ellipse: 0.05 0.04330127 focus }
  profile gb on b.A { ellipse: 0.05 0.04330127 focus }

  mesh ga gb

  drive a at 30 rpm
}
